# tools/ales_library_merge_sheet_witness.rish — the merge verdict as a readable page: render_sheet(merge, out) draws a
# `format lotus-library-merge-sheet-v1` page from an ALES234 Merge (ALES236).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES236.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-150804_fill-ales236-lotus-library-merge-sheet.md
# Idiom kin: lotus/library_merge.rye (ALES234, the Merge this reads) · lotus/library_diff_sheet.rye (ALES230, the readable-projection
# idiom this mirrors) · lotus/library_manifest.rye (ALES226, the catalogs the merge reconciled). A projection, not a round-trip — no
# new frame, no new hash.
#
# lotus/library_merge_sheet.rye renders a Merge as a readable page. The selftest proves: the page opens with the format line and a
# summary line carrying the three tallies (the summary law); every resolved album appears with its source word and digest (the
# resolved law); every dropped album appears with the source that removed it (the dropped law); every conflict appears with its name
# and three digest fields, an absent side printed `-` (the conflict law); a too-small buffer refuses Overflow (the atomic law). A pure
# text render only — no real file, no DAC, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_library_merge_sheet_witness.rish

say "ales-library-merge-sheet: Language EN — ALES236 draws the ALES234 merge verdict as a readable page: a summary line then one resolved / dropped / conflict line per album, so a keeper sees at a glance which records the merge takes and from whose hand, which it drops, and exactly where the two sides disagree."
say "ales-library-merge-sheet: Style Radiant — a projection, not a round-trip; the eye's version of the merge, every catalog primitive earning its readable page; an absent side in a conflict prints `-`, a token a 64-hex digest can never be, so every conflict line keeps four positional fields; the summary, resolved, dropped, conflict, and atomic laws each proven on metal."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/library_merge_sheet"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/library_merge_sheet.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-library-merge-sheet: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/library_merge_sheet.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-library-merge-sheet: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-library-merge-sheet: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-library-merge-sheet' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-library-merge-sheet: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-library-merge-sheet line"

say "ales-library-merge-sheet: GREEN — the merge verdict reads as a page a keeper can accept at a glance or take to the ear a conflict wants; the merge tier now carries its readable page at the library level. The next Lotus crux still names who made it — a signed carry (crypto seam) on Keaton's word."
